1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,649
A CSS fix from an AI is still a guess until a browser tests it.

2
00:00:04,649 --> 00:00:07,439
BREAK IT turns that guess into a reproducible experiment.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,797
Start with a static component you own.

4
00:00:14,797 --> 00:00:18,393
This checkout looks correct with its short sample copy.

5
00:00:18,393 --> 00:00:21,191
BREAK IT finds text and control slots.

6
00:00:21,191 --> 00:00:28,383
You review the required content and layout rules before running
anything: no overflow, no clipping, and visible, separated

7
00:00:28,383 --> 00:00:28,783
buttons.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,120
Muse Spark one point three creates adversarial content and viewport
combinations, including compound Spanish names, long payment
identifiers,

9
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:40,375
expanded disclosures, and narrow phones.

10
00:00:40,375 --> 00:00:44,435
This recording used a fresh Meta Model API request.

11
00:00:44,435 --> 00:00:48,043
The eight generated cases join one unchanged baseline.

12
00:00:48,043 --> 00:00:50,750
Here are the real response receipts.

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,786
Playwright Chromium now renders all nine cases.

14
00:00:54,786 --> 00:00:57,572
The model does not assign the grades.

15
00:00:57,572 --> 00:01:02,348
The browser measures element bounds, text clipping, control overlap, and
button visibility.

16
00:01:02,348 --> 00:01:03,542
Seven scenarios fail.

17
00:01:03,542 --> 00:01:09,512
In this example a customer name pushes content outside a three hundred
sixty pixel viewport.

18
00:01:09,512 --> 00:01:12,696
Every failure has measurements and a real capture.

19
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,883
We send Muse Spark the component, the failing measurements, and three
actual screenshots.

20
00:01:18,883 --> 00:01:21,887
It proposes CSS and explains the suspected causes.

21
00:01:21,887 --> 00:01:24,141
You can inspect the complete diff.

22
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:26,770
The HTML and test pack remain locked.

23
00:01:26,770 --> 00:01:33,531
If you edit the proposed CSS, the app changes its attribution so your
edits cannot masquerade as untouched

24
00:01:33,531 --> 00:01:34,282
model output.

25
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,032
Now replay the candidate against exactly the same inputs and assertions.

26
00:01:42,032 --> 00:01:46,797
This run improves from two of nine scenarios passing to nine of nine.

27
00:01:46,797 --> 00:01:50,462
Twenty five assertion failures are fixed, with no measured regression.

28
00:01:50,462 --> 00:01:53,394
The matching pack hash makes the comparison auditable.

29
00:01:53,394 --> 00:01:59,625
Passing means these declared layout checks passed on these cases, not
that the application is universally correct.

30
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:09,111
Export the patch with its original component, locked scenarios,
assertion logs, before and after screenshots, and model receipts.

31
00:02:09,111 --> 00:02:15,038
The ZIP includes a standalone Chromium runner compiled from the same
evaluator as the app.

32
00:02:15,038 --> 00:02:20,964
Another developer can reproduce the failures and the candidate result
without making another model call.

33
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:29,667
Built during this hackathon: slot instrumentation, Meta orchestration,
layout checks, locked replay, and proof export.

34
00:02:29,667 --> 00:02:31,933
React and Chromium are existing tools.

35
00:02:31,933 --> 00:02:36,844
A separate boarding pass improved from zero of nine to nine of nine.

36
00:02:36,844 --> 00:02:38,733
These are intentionally fragile examples.

37
00:02:38,733 --> 00:02:41,000
The public repository includes replayable evidence.
